{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB12141 - MONDO_18433",
  "verbalization": "Gilteritinib, a multitargeted tyrosine kinase inhibitor, is shown to have a clinically established indication for Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation and Without Maturation, both of which are closely related to Acute Myeloid Leukemia With T(6;9)(p23;q34). The shared indication for Aldesleukin, a cytokine used in the treatment of leukemia, suggests a potential pathway involving immune modulation. Gilteritinib's mechanism of action, which includes inhibition of multiple kinases, may influence cellular processes critical to leukemogenesis, such as cell proliferation and survival. The presence of a shared pathway involving Idarubicin, another chemotherapy agent, further supports a mechanistic link, though the specific role of Gilteritinib in this context remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence suggests a possible role for Gilteritinib in the treatment of this leukemia subtype, though further studies are needed to confirm its therapeutic potential.",
